IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ssa-claims`

-- Social Security Act 1991 (Cth), ss 7(5), 17, 23, 43, 44, 94, 103, 197, 198, 540-543B, 593, 598, 954, 954A, 957A, 1061ZG, 1064, 1067G, 1071, 1166-1173 -- worked cases.
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Nothing here has been checked
-- against a decided case or subsidiary legislation (none was retrieved).
--
-- Fixtures are generated from a table of which flags are set (scratchpad gen_social-security-act-1991_baseline.py). Expected results were written by
-- hand from the text.

-- ILLUSTRATIVE INPUTS. The Act does not state the maximum rates, assets value limits, income free areas or income limits in force
-- on the scenario dates (all are indexed), and the deposit has LOST the reduction formulas of point 1064-E10 and Table G-2, of
-- 1067G-F28, s 598(2A) and s 1170(4). Every such figure below is an illustrative input chosen by the encoder, not a figure read in the
-- deposit: maximum rates of $20,000 a year (single) and $15,000 (each member of a couple); the Table G-1 and E-1 base figures used as if
-- in force (the reductions are the Act's own, from the formulas transcribed in the sidecar: 50c per dollar of income excess and
-- $19.50 per $250 of assets excess, each member of a couple on a 50/50 share); a seniors health card income limit of $72,000 (the
-- printed base for a couple); a parental income free area of $55,000; for F28, maximum payment rates of $450 (the person) and $700
-- (everyone with the parental income in common); and income cut-out components of $23,000, $1,800, $366 and $1,668 a year, which
-- give $1,000 a week. The scenarios give NO asset or income figures for any of the lives; the values
-- below are invented to show the shape of the outcome each scenario records. The cases test the encoding, not the lives' outcomes.

